Third-party material
--------------------

This repository carries none, with one exception, and it is the file
next door. LICENSE is the Free Software Foundation's text of the AGPL,
reproduced verbatim: it carries its own permission -- copy and
distribute it verbatim, changing it is not allowed -- so it is the
licence rather than material licensed by it, and it is not offered under
the grant above.

Everything else in a checkout is written for this repository and covered
by that grant. Nothing is forked, nothing is vendored, and nothing is
copied in from elsewhere: no upstream code under other terms, no seeded
database dump, not even an Ansible collection -- the role uses "assert"
and "include_role" from ansible.builtin and nothing else. The check
scripts read jinja2 and PyYAML; they import them, they do not carry
them, and those libraries reach you from their own distributor under
their own terms rather than from here.

What this repository DEPLOYS is a different question, and it is the one
that grows.

Today the answer is still none. "services:" in the stack's template,
roles/deploy_AIS_Docker_stack/templates/AIS.docker-compose.yml.j2, is an
empty mapping, so a run of this role names no image and pulls none: the
stack is the shape services are added into, and the first service issue
has not landed.

The compose file is where container images are named, and the
orchestrator pulls each named image from that image's own registry at
deployment time. They are not carried here, not redistributed here, and
not covered by anything here. They are not covered works of this
repository and this repository never distributes them, so nothing here
places them under the AGPL: it governs this work and works based on it,
and an image fetched from its own registry at deployment time is
neither. This repository grants no rights over any of them, and could
not: they are not its to grant. Every one of them keeps its own licence
entirely, whatever that licence is -- and complying with it is the
business of whoever deploys the stack, not something this repository
does or can do on their behalf.

No image is named in this file, and the template stays the place where
they are named: each arrives there pinned by version, which is the
authoritative record of what the stack pulls, and a second list here
would drift away from it at the first bump. The exception would be an
image whose own terms require a notice to travel alongside the software,
which is what a file like this one is for.

The role that deploys the stack is not here either.
"AIS_Docker_stack_deployment_role_name" names it, the caller supplies
it, and it is resolved from the caller's own roles_path. It belongs to
whoever wrote it, under whatever terms they chose. That the stack can be
deployed by a role this repository does not contain is the design rather
than an omission, and it is why nothing in this file can speak for that
role's licence.


